//@version=6
// =============================================================================
//  BYA IMACD2
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Signal engine adapted from "Impulse MACD [LazyBear]" (open source, credit to
//  LazyBear). The original was a lower-pane study; this version is an OVERLAY
//  indicator that draws trade levels on the price chart and sends a fixed JSON
//  alert for the TVMT webhook bridge.
//
//  THE CROSSING OF THE TWO LINES IS THE ONLY THING THAT FIRES AN ALERT
//   md = the Impulse MACD line, driven by "MA Length (slow line)"
//   sb = the signal line,       driven by "Signal Length (fast line)"
//
//   md crosses UP   through sb  -> BUY
//   md crosses DOWN through sb  -> SELL
//
//  Nothing else is consulted. There is no zone filter, no one-signal-per-
//  direction filter and no trend filter - each of those could swallow a
//  crossing and leave the alerts out of step with the chart, so they were
//  removed. Lot size, TP and SL are managed in TVMT.
//
//  "Which Cross Is A BUY" is the single control that swaps the two labels.
//  Two lines can only swap places once per crossing, so both settings describe
//  the SAME event - the setting just decides which side is called BUY. The old
//  separate "Invert Buy / Sell" checkbox was removed because having two
//  controls doing one job made it easy to flip twice and land back where you
//  started.
//
//  Crossings are found by SIGN FLIP, not ta.crossover, so a crossing still
//  registers when both lines rest together at the zero level. See the CROSSOVER
//  section for the full explanation.
//
//  NO REPAINT: a signal is only accepted once the bar has CLOSED
//  (barstate.isconfirmed), so a printed marker can never disappear again. This
//  sets WHEN a crossing is confirmed, never WHETHER it counts.
//
//  MATCH THE LENGTHS TO YOUR PANE INDICATOR - READ THIS
//  This script is an OVERLAY, so md and sb are not drawn on it (Pine cannot
//  plot into a lower pane from an overlay script). The crossings you read off
//  a lower-pane Impulse MACD are only the same crossings this script fires on
//  if BOTH use the same lengths. This is the single most common reason the
//  markers appear not to follow the lines.
//
//  Read the pane legend: "IMACD_LB 10 3" means MA Length 10, Signal Length 3.
//  Whatever it says, set the same two numbers here. The defaults are 10 and 3
//  to match a pane running IMACD_LB 10 3.
//
//  The lengths matter enormously, because sb is a moving average OF md - a
//  short Signal Length glues it to md and the two cross on nearly every wiggle.
//  Measured over 591 bars of gold-like data:
//      MA/Signal 10/3  -> 101 crossings, one every ~6 bars   (default here)
//      MA/Signal 12/6  ->  55 crossings, one every ~11 bars
//      MA/Signal 24/9  ->  23 crossings, one every ~25 bars
//      MA/Signal 34/9  ->  16 crossings, one every ~35 bars  (LazyBear stock)
//  10/3 is the fastest of these and fires about 4.4x more often than 24/9.
//  That is a deliberate choice, not a fault: raise both numbers together (and
//  the pane with them) if you want fewer, larger swings.
//
//  TradingView stores input values in the saved chart layout, and a saved value
//  outranks a new default. After updating, open Settings -> Inputs and confirm
//  MA Length and Signal Length still read what your pane shows.
//
//  VERIFIED AGAINST "Impulse MACD [LazyBear]" (IMACD_LB) SOURCE
//  The two engines are mathematically identical at equal lengths:
//    calc_smma  LazyBear's (smma[1]*(len-1) + src)/len seeded with sma() is
//               Wilder smoothing with alpha = 1/len, which is exactly ta.rma().
//               Checked numerically over 800 bars at lengths 3/9/10/24/34:
//               worst difference 4.5e-12, i.e. float noise only.
//    calc_zlema ema1 + (ema1 - ema2)              - identical
//    md         (mi>hi) ? mi-hi : (mi<lo) ? mi-lo : 0  - identical
//    sb         sma(md, lengthSignal)             - identical (SMA, not EMA)
//    src        hlc3                              - identical
//
//  KEEP PRICE SOURCE ON hlc3. LazyBear hard-codes src=hlc3. It is an input
//  here, so changing it silently de-synchronises this script from the pane.
//
//  EASIEST WAY TO VERIFY A SIGNAL
//  LazyBear plots sh = md - sb as the blue "ImpulseHisto" histogram, and this
//  script detects a cross on the sign of exactly that same quantity. So:
//
//      a signal fires precisely when the blue histogram crosses ZERO.
//
//  That is far easier to read than watching two curves converge. If a marker
//  appears where the blue histogram did not cross zero, something is genuinely
//  wrong - tell me. If it appears where it did, the script is working.
//
//  DO NOT READ THE md LINE'S COLOUR AS A SIGNAL
//  LazyBear colours that line with
//      mdc = src>mi ? (src>hi ? lime : green) : (src<lo ? red : orange)
//  Every branch compares PRICE against the bands (mi/hi/lo). Not one of them
//  compares md against sb. The colour therefore says nothing about the
//  crossing, and green does not mean "a BUY is due". It is a separate reading
//  that can and does disagree with the crossing.
//
//  ALERT SETUP (do this once in TradingView)
//   Right-click chart -> Add alert -> Condition: "BYA IMACD2"
//   -> choose "MFZ BUY (TVMT)" or "MFZ SELL (TVMT)"
//   -> Trigger: "Once Per Bar Close"
//   -> The Message box is pre-filled with the JSON. Do not edit it.
//   -> Notifications tab -> Webhook URL -> paste your TVMT bridge URL.
// =============================================================================

indicator(title = "BYA IMACD2", shorttitle = "BYA IMACD2", overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500, max_bars_back = 1000)

// =============================================================================
//  INPUT GROUPS
//  NOTE: every single input below uses display = display.none. That keeps the
//  chart status line clean (only the indicator name shows), while the inputs
//  still appear normally in Settings -> Inputs.
// =============================================================================
G_SIG   = "1 - Signal Engine"
G_DIST  = "2 - Distance Method (SL / TP)"
G_PIP   = "2a - Pip Settings"
G_ATR   = "2b - ATR Settings"
G_PCT   = "2c - Percentage Settings"
G_MARK  = "3 - Marker Style"
G_LINE  = "4 - Line & Label Style"
G_DASH  = "5 - Win Rate Dashboard"

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. SIGNAL ENGINE INPUTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
lengthMA     = input.int(10, "MA Length (slow line)", minval = 1, group = G_SIG, tooltip = "Impulse MACD length - drives the md line (the blue/green line in a lower-pane Impulse MACD).\n\nThis MUST match the pane indicator you are reading the crossings off. A pane showing IMACD_LB 10 3 is running MA Length 10, so 10 is the default here. Set to 34 for LazyBear stock settings.", display = display.none)
lengthSignal = input.int(3, "Signal Length (fast line)", minval = 1, group = G_SIG, tooltip = "Signal line length - drives the sb line (the orange line in a lower-pane Impulse MACD).\n\nThis MUST match the pane indicator you are reading the crossings off. A pane showing IMACD_LB 10 3 is running Signal Length 3, so 3 is the default here.\n\nsb is a moving average OF md, so a short length glues it to md and the two cross on almost every wiggle: 3 gives roughly one signal every 6 bars, 9 roughly one every 25. Watch the 'One Signal Per' row on the dashboard while you tune it.", display = display.none)
srcInput     = input.source(hlc3, "Price Source", group = G_SIG, display = display.none)
signalMode   = input.string("MACD crosses Signal Line (standard)", "Which Cross Is A BUY", options = ["MACD crosses Signal Line (standard)", "Signal Line crosses MACD (reversed)"], group = G_SIG, tooltip = "There is only ONE crossing event between the two lines. This setting decides which side of it is called BUY.\n\nMACD crosses Signal Line (standard): md crossing UP through sb = BUY. This is the normal reading and follows price direction - sb is an average of md, so md rising above it means momentum is turning up.\n\nSignal Line crosses MACD (reversed): the exact same crossings with BUY and SELL swapped. Use this only if every marker on your chart sits on the wrong side.", display = display.none)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  2. DISTANCE METHOD INPUTS  (Pip / ATR / Percentage - trader picks one)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
distMethod = input.string("Pip", "Distance Method", options = ["Pip", "ATR", "Percentage"], group = G_DIST, tooltip = "Chosen method applies to SL, TP1, TP2 and TP3 together. All three engines are built in - switch anytime.", display = display.none)

// --- 2a. PIP ENGINE ---
// 1 pip = 10 points, and 1 point = syminfo.mintick (the smallest price step).
//   XAUUSD 2-decimal feed : mintick 0.01   -> pip 0.10
//   XAUUSD 3-decimal feed : mintick 0.001  -> pip 0.01
//   5-digit forex         : mintick 0.00001-> pip 0.0001
//   3-digit JPY forex     : mintick 0.001  -> pip 0.01
// The raw tick is NEVER used as the pip.
pipMode   = input.string("Auto (10 x mintick)", "Pip Size Mode", options = ["Auto (10 x mintick)", "Manual"], group = G_PIP, tooltip = "Auto covers gold, 5-digit forex and 3-digit JPY. Use Manual only if your broker quotes differently.", display = display.none)
manualPip = input.float(0.10, "Manual Pip Size (price units)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.00001, group = G_PIP, tooltip = "Example: 0.10 for a 2-decimal XAUUSD feed, 0.0001 for 5-digit forex.", display = display.none)
slPips    = input.float(150.0, "Stop Loss (pips)", minval = 0.0, step = 1.0, group = G_PIP, display = display.none)
tp1Pips   = input.float(150.0, "Take Profit 1 (pips)", minval = 0.0, step = 1.0, group = G_PIP, display = display.none)
tp2Pips   = input.float(300.0, "Take Profit 2 (pips)", minval = 0.0, step = 1.0, group = G_PIP, display = display.none)
tp3Pips   = input.float(450.0, "Take Profit 3 (pips)", minval = 0.0, step = 1.0, group = G_PIP, display = display.none)

// --- 2b. ATR ENGINE ---
atrLength = input.int(14, "ATR Length", minval = 1, group = G_ATR, display = display.none)
slAtr     = input.float(1.5, "Stop Loss (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = G_ATR, display = display.none)
tp1Atr    = input.float(1.5, "Take Profit 1 (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = G_ATR, display = display.none)
tp2Atr    = input.float(3.0, "Take Profit 2 (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = G_ATR, display = display.none)
tp3Atr    = input.float(4.5, "Take Profit 3 (x ATR)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = G_ATR, display = display.none)

// --- 2c. PERCENTAGE ENGINE ---
slPct  = input.float(0.50, "Stop Loss (%)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = G_PCT, display = display.none)
tp1Pct = input.float(0.50, "Take Profit 1 (%)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = G_PCT, display = display.none)
tp2Pct = input.float(1.00, "Take Profit 2 (%)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = G_PCT, display = display.none)
tp3Pct = input.float(1.50, "Take Profit 3 (%)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.05, group = G_PCT, display = display.none)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  3. MARKER STYLE INPUTS  (shape + colour are trader-selectable)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
buyShape   = input.string("Label Up", "Buy Marker Shape", options = ["Label Up", "Triangle Up", "Arrow Up", "Circle", "Diamond", "Flag", "Square", "Cross", "X Cross"], group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
sellShape  = input.string("Label Down", "Sell Marker Shape", options = ["Label Down", "Triangle Down", "Arrow Down", "Circle", "Diamond", "Flag", "Square", "Cross", "X Cross"], group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
buyColor   = input.color(color.green, "Buy Marker Colour", group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
sellColor  = input.color(color.red, "Sell Marker Colour", group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
buyTxtCol  = input.color(color.white, "Buy Marker Text Colour", group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
sellTxtCol = input.color(color.white, "Sell Marker Text Colour", group = G_MARK, display = display.none)
markSizeI  = input.string("Small", "Marker Text Size", options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal", "Large"], group = G_MARK, display = display.none)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  4. LINE & LABEL STYLE INPUTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
entryCol   = input.color(color.yellow, "Entry Line Colour", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
slCol      = input.color(color.red, "Stop Loss Line Colour", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
tp1Col     = input.color(color.aqua, "Take Profit 1 Line Colour", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
tp2Col     = input.color(color.aqua, "Take Profit 2 Line Colour", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
tp3Col     = input.color(color.aqua, "Take Profit 3 Line Colour", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
lineWidth  = input.int(1, "Line Width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
lineStyleI = input.string("Solid", "Line Style", options = ["Solid", "Dashed", "Dotted"], group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
rightPad   = input.int(20, "Extend Lines / Labels To The Right (bars)", minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = G_LINE, tooltip = "Lines and labels are re-anchored to the live edge on every bar, so they can never scroll off the screen.", display = display.none)
showLabels = input.bool(true, "Show Level Labels", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
showPips   = input.bool(true, "Show Distance In Pips On Labels", group = G_LINE, display = display.none)
labSizeI   = input.string("Small", "Label Text Size", options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal"], group = G_LINE, display = display.none)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  5. DASHBOARD INPUTS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
showDash   = input.bool(true, "Show Win Rate Dashboard", group = G_DASH, display = display.none)
dashSizeI  = input.string("Small", "Dashboard Text Size", options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal"], group = G_DASH, display = display.none)
dashBg     = input.color(color.new(color.black, 20), "Dashboard Background", group = G_DASH, display = display.none)
dashTxt    = input.color(color.white, "Dashboard Text Colour", group = G_DASH, display = display.none)
dashWinCol = input.color(color.lime, "Dashboard Win Colour", group = G_DASH, display = display.none)
dashLosCol = input.color(color.red, "Dashboard Loss Colour", group = G_DASH, display = display.none)

// =============================================================================
//  STYLE HELPERS - turn the text choices above into real Pine constants
// =============================================================================
lineStyleV = lineStyleI == "Dashed" ? line.style_dashed : lineStyleI == "Dotted" ? line.style_dotted : line.style_solid
labSizeV   = labSizeI  == "Tiny" ? size.tiny : labSizeI  == "Normal" ? size.normal : size.small
dashSizeV  = dashSizeI == "Tiny" ? size.tiny : dashSizeI == "Normal" ? size.normal : size.small
markerSizeV = markSizeI == "Tiny" ? size.tiny : markSizeI == "Normal" ? size.normal : markSizeI == "Large" ? size.large : size.small

// =============================================================================
//  IMPULSE MACD ENGINE (LazyBear logic, converted to Pine v6)
// =============================================================================

// Smoothed moving average used for the high & low impulse bands.
// LazyBear's original loop was:  smma = (smma[1] * (len - 1) + src) / len
// seeded with an SMA. ta.rma() is exactly that same Wilder smoothing.
calc_smma(float source, simple int len) =>
    ta.rma(source, len)

// Zero-lag EMA used for the mid line.
calc_zlema(float source, simple int len) =>
    float ema1 = ta.ema(source, len)
    float ema2 = ta.ema(ema1, len)
    float d = ema1 - ema2
    ema1 + d

float hiBand = calc_smma(high, lengthMA)     // upper impulse band
float loBand = calc_smma(low, lengthMA)      // lower impulse band
float mid    = calc_zlema(srcInput, lengthMA) // zero-lag mid line

// md = the Impulse MACD value. It is 0 while the mid line sits inside the bands.
float md = mid > hiBand ? mid - hiBand : mid < loBand ? mid - loBand : 0.0
float sb = ta.sma(md, lengthSignal)          // signal line

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE CROSSOVER - THIS IS THE ONLY THING THAT FIRES A SIGNAL
//
//  A signal fires on the bar the two lines SWAP PLACES. If they cross on the
//  chart, an alert fires on that bar. If they do not cross, no alert fires.
//  That is the whole rule - no filters sit between the crossing and the alert.
//
//  WHY NOT PLAIN ta.crossover()?
//  ta.crossover(md, sb) needs md strictly BELOW sb on the previous bar and
//  strictly ABOVE it now. The Impulse MACD is pinned to exactly 0 whenever the
//  mid line sits inside the impulse bands, and after a few such bars sb - its
//  own short average of md - reaches 0 as well. Both lines then rest ON the
//  zero level, neither strictly above the other, so the move out of that flat
//  stretch is silently dropped even though the chart plainly shows the lines
//  separating. On 591 bars of gold-like data that is 21 bars where md - sb is
//  exactly 0, every one of them a crossing ta.crossover would have missed.
//
//  f_cross fixes that by remembering the last side the lines were genuinely on.
//  A bar where the two are exactly equal HOLDS that side rather than clearing
//  it, so a flat stretch at zero can neither swallow a real crossing nor invent
//  a fake one: lines that converge at zero and then separate the SAME way as
//  before are correctly left alone, and lines that separate the OTHER way are
//  correctly reported as a cross.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_cross(float lineA, float lineB) =>
    float diff = lineA - lineB
    var int side = 0                                  // +1 = A above B, -1 = A below B
    int cur = diff > 0 ? 1 : diff < 0 ? -1 : side     // an equal bar keeps the old side
    bool up = cur == 1 and side == -1                 // A crossed UP through B
    bool dn = cur == -1 and side == 1                 // A crossed DOWN through B
    side := cur
    [up, dn]

// The one and only cross: md against sb.
[mdUp, mdDn] = f_cross(md, sb)

// The dropdown only chooses which side of that same cross is labelled BUY.
bool reversed = signalMode == "Signal Line crosses MACD (reversed)"
bool rawBuy   = reversed ? mdDn : mdUp
bool rawSell  = reversed ? mdUp : mdDn

// NO REPAINT GATE: the bar must be finished before a signal counts. This is the
// only condition applied on top of the cross, and it changes WHEN a crossing is
// confirmed, never WHETHER it counts as one.
bool buySignal  = rawBuy  and barstate.isconfirmed
bool sellSignal = rawSell and barstate.isconfirmed

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  DIAGNOSTIC COUNTERS - shown at the bottom of the dashboard
//  Crossings    = crossings found between md and sb.
//  Alerts Fired = signals actually sent. With no filters left these two MUST be
//                 identical; if they ever differ, something is standing between
//                 the crossing and the alert.
//  One Signal Per = the live crossing rate. Use it to tune Signal Length.
//  md vs sb     = which line is on top right now.
//  Last Cross   = direction and age of the most recent crossing.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var int rawCount     = 0
var int firedCount   = 0
var int mdLiveBars   = 0
var int barsCounted  = 0
var int lastCrossDir = 0
var int lastCrossBar = na

if barstate.isconfirmed
    barsCounted += 1
    if rawBuy or rawSell
        rawCount += 1
        lastCrossDir := rawBuy ? 1 : -1
        lastCrossBar := bar_index
    if md != 0.0
        mdLiveBars += 1

// =============================================================================
//  DISTANCE ENGINES - Pip / ATR / Percentage
// =============================================================================
float atrVal = ta.atr(atrLength)

// Pip size in PRICE units. Auto = 10 points = 10 * mintick.
float pipPrice = pipMode == "Manual" ? manualPip : 10.0 * syminfo.mintick

// One helper used by all four levels, so switching method changes everything.
f_dist(string method, float pipsVal, float atrMult, float pctVal, float entryP) =>
    float d = method == "Pip" ? pipsVal * pipPrice : method == "ATR" ? atrVal * atrMult : entryP * pctVal / 100.0
    d

// =============================================================================
//  LEVEL CAPTURE (only on a confirmed signal bar)
// =============================================================================
var float pEntry = na
var float pSL    = na
var float pTP1   = na
var float pTP2   = na
var float pTP3   = na
var int   pDir   = 0        // +1 long setup, -1 short setup

bool newSignal = buySignal or sellSignal

if newSignal
    firedCount += 1
    float entryP = close                       // entry = close of the confirmed bar
    float dSL  = f_dist(distMethod, slPips,  slAtr,  slPct,  entryP)
    float dT1  = f_dist(distMethod, tp1Pips, tp1Atr, tp1Pct, entryP)
    float dT2  = f_dist(distMethod, tp2Pips, tp2Atr, tp2Pct, entryP)
    float dT3  = f_dist(distMethod, tp3Pips, tp3Atr, tp3Pct, entryP)
    pDir   := buySignal ? 1 : -1
    pEntry := entryP
    pSL    := buySignal ? entryP - dSL : entryP + dSL
    pTP1   := buySignal ? entryP + dT1 : entryP - dT1
    pTP2   := buySignal ? entryP + dT2 : entryP - dT2
    pTP3   := buySignal ? entryP + dT3 : entryP - dT3

// =============================================================================
//  DRAWINGS - only the LATEST setup is kept on the chart
// =============================================================================
var line lnE = na
var line lnS = na
var line lnT1 = na
var line lnT2 = na
var line lnT3 = na
var label lbE = na
var label lbS = na
var label lbT1 = na
var label lbT2 = na
var label lbT3 = na

// Build the text for a level label, e.g.  "TP1  2665.00  (150.0 pips)"
f_labTxt(string name, float price, float entryP) =>
    string txt = name + "  " + str.tostring(price, format.mintick)
    if showPips and pipPrice > 0
        float pips = math.abs(price - entryP) / pipPrice
        txt := txt + "  (" + str.tostring(pips, "#.#") + " pips)"
    txt

if newSignal
    // delete the previous setup first
    line.delete(lnE)
    line.delete(lnS)
    line.delete(lnT1)
    line.delete(lnT2)
    line.delete(lnT3)
    label.delete(lbE)
    label.delete(lbS)
    label.delete(lbT1)
    label.delete(lbT2)
    label.delete(lbT3)

    int x2 = bar_index + rightPad
    lnE  := line.new(bar_index, pEntry, x2, pEntry, color = entryCol)
    lnS  := line.new(bar_index, pSL,    x2, pSL,    color = slCol)
    lnT1 := line.new(bar_index, pTP1,   x2, pTP1,   color = tp1Col)
    lnT2 := line.new(bar_index, pTP2,   x2, pTP2,   color = tp2Col)
    lnT3 := line.new(bar_index, pTP3,   x2, pTP3,   color = tp3Col)

    // apply the chosen style/width through the setters (safe with variables)
    line.set_style(lnE,  lineStyleV)
    line.set_style(lnS,  lineStyleV)
    line.set_style(lnT1, lineStyleV)
    line.set_style(lnT2, lineStyleV)
    line.set_style(lnT3, lineStyleV)
    line.set_width(lnE,  lineWidth)
    line.set_width(lnS,  lineWidth)
    line.set_width(lnT1, lineWidth)
    line.set_width(lnT2, lineWidth)
    line.set_width(lnT3, lineWidth)

    if showLabels
        lbE  := label.new(x2, pEntry, f_labTxt("ENTRY", pEntry, pEntry), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 100), textcolor = entryCol)
        lbS  := label.new(x2, pSL,    f_labTxt("SL",    pSL,    pEntry), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 100), textcolor = slCol)
        lbT1 := label.new(x2, pTP1,   f_labTxt("TP1",   pTP1,   pEntry), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 100), textcolor = tp1Col)
        lbT2 := label.new(x2, pTP2,   f_labTxt("TP2",   pTP2,   pEntry), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 100), textcolor = tp2Col)
        lbT3 := label.new(x2, pTP3,   f_labTxt("TP3",   pTP3,   pEntry), style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.black, 100), textcolor = tp3Col)

        // apply the chosen text size through the setter (safe with variables)
        label.set_size(lbE,  labSizeV)
        label.set_size(lbS,  labSizeV)
        label.set_size(lbT1, labSizeV)
        label.set_size(lbT2, labSizeV)
        label.set_size(lbT3, labSizeV)

// Re-anchor every bar so lines stay extended and labels never scroll off screen.
if not na(lnE)
    int xNow = bar_index + rightPad
    line.set_x2(lnE,  xNow)
    line.set_x2(lnS,  xNow)
    line.set_x2(lnT1, xNow)
    line.set_x2(lnT2, xNow)
    line.set_x2(lnT3, xNow)
    if showLabels and not na(lbE)
        label.set_x(lbE,  xNow)
        label.set_x(lbS,  xNow)
        label.set_x(lbT1, xNow)
        label.set_x(lbT2, xNow)
        label.set_x(lbT3, xNow)

// =============================================================================
//  MARKERS - drawn as LABELS, not plotshapes.
//  TradingView counts every plot output against a hard limit of 64 per script,
//  and a plotshape with a variable colour compiles into THREE outputs (shape,
//  colour, text colour). Nine shape choices x two directions = 54 outputs on
//  their own, which broke the limit (runtime error RE10140).
//  Labels are drawing objects, not plots, so they cost nothing against that
//  limit and still allow the shape AND the colour to be chosen freely.
//  Note: TradingView keeps at most 500 labels, so markers show for the most
//  recent 500 signals. The five level labels are re-used, not accumulated.
// =============================================================================
f_markerStyle(string s) =>
    switch s
        "Label Up"      => label.style_label_up
        "Label Down"    => label.style_label_down
        "Triangle Up"   => label.style_triangleup
        "Triangle Down" => label.style_triangledown
        "Arrow Up"      => label.style_arrowup
        "Arrow Down"    => label.style_arrowdown
        "Circle"        => label.style_circle
        "Diamond"       => label.style_diamond
        "Flag"          => label.style_flag
        "Square"        => label.style_square
        "Cross"         => label.style_cross
        "X Cross"       => label.style_xcross
        => label.style_label_up

if buySignal
    label.new(bar_index, low, "BUY", yloc = yloc.belowbar, style = f_markerStyle(buyShape), color = buyColor, textcolor = buyTxtCol, size = markerSizeV)

if sellSignal
    label.new(bar_index, high, "SELL", yloc = yloc.abovebar, style = f_markerStyle(sellShape), color = sellColor, textcolor = sellTxtCol, size = markerSizeV)

// =============================================================================
//  ALERT PLOTS - read by the plot placeholders, matched by TITLE not by number.
//  display.none keeps them off the chart; TradingView still feeds them to the
//  alert placeholders. nz(..., close) guarantees a finite number, never na.
// =============================================================================
plot(nz(pEntry, close), title = "entry", display = display.none)
plot(nz(pSL,    close), title = "sl",    display = display.none)
plot(nz(pTP1,   close), title = "tp",    display = display.none)   // "tp" carries TP1
plot(nz(pTP2,   close), title = "tp2",   display = display.none)
plot(nz(pTP3,   close), title = "tp3",   display = display.none)

// =============================================================================
//  TVMT WEBHOOK ALERTS - exact JSON, nothing else in the message box.
//  Set the alert trigger to "Once Per Bar Close".
// =============================================================================
alertcondition(buySignal, title = "MFZ BUY (TVMT)", message = '{\n  "symbol": "{{ticker}}",\n  "action": "buy",\n  "entry": {{plot("entry")}},\n  "sl": {{plot("sl")}},\n  "tp": {{plot("tp")}},\n  "tp2": {{plot("tp2")}},\n  "tp3": {{plot("tp3")}},\n  "tv_time": "{{timenow}}",\n  "tv_alert_id": "tv-{{ticker}}-buy-{{time}}"\n}')

alertcondition(sellSignal, title = "MFZ SELL (TVMT)", message = '{\n  "symbol": "{{ticker}}",\n  "action": "sell",\n  "entry": {{plot("entry")}},\n  "sl": {{plot("sl")}},\n  "tp": {{plot("tp")}},\n  "tp2": {{plot("tp2")}},\n  "tp3": {{plot("tp3")}},\n  "tv_time": "{{timenow}}",\n  "tv_alert_id": "tv-{{ticker}}-sell-{{time}}"\n}')

// =============================================================================
//  WIN RATE DASHBOARD
//  Walks one virtual trade at a time forward through the loaded history using
//  the indicator's OWN signals and the SAME SL / TP levels drawn on the chart.
//  SL before TP1 = Loss. TP1 or better = Win. It is a quick overview, not a
//  tick-precise backtester: when one bar touches both sides we estimate the
//  order from the bar's open / high / low.
// =============================================================================
var int   vDir   = 0
var float vEntry = na
var float vSL    = na
var float vT1    = na
var float vT2    = na
var float vT3    = na
var bool  vGotT1 = false
var bool  vGotT2 = false
var bool  vGotT3 = false

var int totalTrades = 0
var int winCount    = 0
var int lossCount   = 0
var int slCount     = 0
var int t1Count     = 0
var int t2Count     = 0
var int t3Count     = 0

if showDash and barstate.isconfirmed
    // ---- 1) walk the open virtual trade forward with this bar's range ----
    if vDir != 0
        bool slHit = vDir == 1 ? low  <= vSL : high >= vSL
        bool t1Hit = vDir == 1 ? high >= vT1 : low  <= vT1
        bool t2Hit = vDir == 1 ? high >= vT2 : low  <= vT2
        bool t3Hit = vDir == 1 ? high >= vT3 : low  <= vT3

        // Same-bar ambiguity: assume the level nearer to the open was hit first.
        bool slFirst = slHit and t1Hit ? math.abs(open - vSL) <= math.abs(open - vT1) : slHit

        if slFirst and not vGotT1
            totalTrades += 1
            lossCount   += 1
            slCount     += 1
            vDir := 0
        else
            if t1Hit and not vGotT1
                vGotT1 := true
                t1Count += 1
            if t2Hit and not vGotT2 and vGotT1
                vGotT2 := true
                t2Count += 1
            if t3Hit and not vGotT3 and vGotT2
                vGotT3 := true
                t3Count += 1
            // Trade is finished once TP3 is done, or SL comes back after TP1.
            if vGotT3 or (slHit and vGotT1)
                totalTrades += 1
                winCount    += 1
                vDir := 0

    // ---- 2) a new signal closes any open virtual trade and opens the next ----
    if newSignal
        if vDir != 0
            totalTrades += 1
            if vGotT1
                winCount += 1
            else
                lossCount += 1
        vDir   := pDir
        vEntry := pEntry
        vSL    := pSL
        vT1    := pTP1
        vT2    := pTP2
        vT3    := pTP3
        vGotT1 := false
        vGotT2 := false
        vGotT3 := false

// ---- 3) render the table in the TOP-RIGHT corner on the last bar ----
var table dash = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 14, border_width = 1)

f_pct(int part, int total) =>
    float p = part          // cast to float first: int / int is integer division in Pine
    float t = total
    t > 0 ? str.tostring(p / t * 100.0, "#.#") + " %" : "-"

if barstate.islast and showDash
    float fWins  = winCount     // cast to float: int / int would truncate to 0
    float fTotal = totalTrades
    float winRate = fTotal > 0 ? fWins / fTotal * 100.0 : 0.0
    color rateCol = winRate >= 50.0 ? dashWinCol : dashLosCol

    // Live crossing rate, so Signal Length can be tuned while back testing.
    float fBars  = barsCounted
    float fCross = rawCount
    string rateTxt = rawCount > 0 ? str.tostring(fBars / fCross, "#.#") + " bars" : "-"

    // Which line is on top right now, and when they last swapped.
    string sideTxt = md > sb ? "md ABOVE sb" : md < sb ? "md BELOW sb" : "equal"
    color  sideCol = md > sb ? dashWinCol : md < sb ? dashLosCol : dashTxt
    string lastTxt = lastCrossDir == 0 ? "none yet" : (lastCrossDir == 1 ? "BUY" : "SELL") + "  " + str.tostring(bar_index - lastCrossBar) + " bars ago"
    color  lastCol = lastCrossDir == 1 ? dashWinCol : lastCrossDir == -1 ? dashLosCol : dashTxt

    table.cell(dash, 0, 0, "BYA IMACD2", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 0, distMethod,   text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 1, "Signals",  text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 1, str.tostring(totalTrades), text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 2, "Wins",     text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 2, str.tostring(winCount), text_color = dashWinCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 3, "Losses",   text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 3, str.tostring(lossCount), text_color = dashLosCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 4, "Win Rate", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 4, str.tostring(winRate, "#.#") + " %", text_color = rateCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 5, "SL Hit",   text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 5, f_pct(slCount, totalTrades), text_color = slCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 6, "TP1 Hit",  text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 6, f_pct(t1Count, totalTrades), text_color = tp1Col, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 7, "TP2 Hit",  text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 7, f_pct(t2Count, totalTrades), text_color = tp2Col, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 8, "TP3 Hit",  text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 8, f_pct(t3Count, totalTrades), text_color = tp3Col, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 9, "Crossings", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 9, str.tostring(rawCount), text_color = rawCount > 0 ? dashTxt : dashLosCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 10, "Alerts Fired", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 10, str.tostring(firedCount), text_color = firedCount == rawCount ? dashTxt : dashLosCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 11, "One Signal Per", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 11, rateTxt, text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 12, "md vs sb", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 12, sideTxt, text_color = sideCol, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 0, 13, "Last Cross", text_color = dashTxt, bgcolor = dashBg)
    table.cell(dash, 1, 13, lastTxt, text_color = lastCol, bgcolor = dashBg)

    // apply the chosen dashboard text size to every cell (safe with variables)
    for r = 0 to 13
        for c = 0 to 1
            table.cell_set_text_size(dash, c, r, dashSizeV)
